---
// Talk version of /blog/nastaliq-distilled. 15 minutes: about 13 for the
// slides, then a live Runebender demo. Images and demos are the post's own.
import NeuralTypeDemo from '@/components/NeuralTypeDemoIsland.astro'
import TraceDemo from '@/components/TraceDemoIsland.astro'
import { baBasicDemo, nastaliqDemo } from '@/components/demo-presets'
import Fig from '@/components/slides/Fig.astro'
import Slide from '@/components/slides/Slide.astro'
import SlidesLayout from '@/layouts/SlidesLayout.astro'

import fatihaLinesCalligraphy from '@/assets/slides/nastaliq-distilled/fatiha-lines-calligraphy-tight.png'
import fatihaLinesGulzar from '@/assets/slides/nastaliq-distilled/fatiha-lines-gulzar-tight.png'
import gulzarInUse from '@/assets/slides/nastaliq-distilled/gulzar-in-use.jpg'
import shapeSorter from '@/assets/slides/nastaliq-distilled/shape-sorter.jpg'
import endRunebender from '@/assets/slides/nastaliq-distilled/end-runebender.jpg'
import titleRunebender from '@/assets/slides/nastaliq-distilled/title-runebender.jpg'
import fatiha from '@/content/blog/nastaliq-distilled/fatiha-gulzar-comparison.png'
import mishkin09 from '@/content/blog/nastaliq-distilled/mishkin-qalam-09.jpg'
import train from '@/content/blog/nastaliq-distilled/train.png'

const PD = 'Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.'
---

<SlidesLayout
  title="Nasta’liq Distilled (slides)"
  description="Talk slides for Nasta’liq Distilled: Post-OpenType Neural Network Fonts."
  running="Nasta’liq Distilled"
  author="Eli Heuer"
>
  <!-- ── Opening ── -->

  <Slide label="Title">
    <img
      class="bleed"
      src={titleRunebender.src}
      alt="Runebender with a bismillah labeled for training: each letter’s ink inside a colored polygon."
    />
    <div class="c-1-9 over-bleed">
      <h1 class="t-display descends">Nasta’liq Distilled</h1>
      <p class="t-head">Post-OpenType<br />Neural Network Fonts</p>
    </div>
    <div class="c-1-6 over-bleed at-bottom">
      <p class="t-body">Eli Heuer 2026</p>
    </div>
    <Fragment slot="notes">
      <p>I make font tools. This talk is about a font that is a neural network.</p>
      <p>Based on the blog post at elih.net/blog/nastaliq-distilled.</p>
    </Fragment>
  </Slide>

  <Slide section="The problem" label="John Hudson on OpenType Layout">
    <img
      class="bleed"
      src={shapeSorter.src}
      alt="A clay bunny pushing a square block toward the round hole of a shape sorter."
    />
    <div class="c-1-7 over-bleed">
      <p class="t-body">
        A dozen or so years ago, I was able to sit down with Eliyezer Kohen,
        one of the original inventors of OpenType Layout, and talk through the
        difficulties of cascading Arabic. He told me something that neither I
        nor any of my colleagues working with Arabic to that date had been
        aware of: he never expected OTL to be able to handle cascading Arabic,
        which he considered to be outside the scope of the technology.
        Unfortunately, this information was lost because Eliyezer had left
        Microsoft before OpenType became widely adopted. As Eliyezer put it to
        me at the end of our conversation, he had been happy in the mid-1990s
        to target 98% of the world with OTL and for Tom Milo to have the rest.
      </p>
      <p class="t-body">
        A lot of time and effort has been wasted over the years trying to
        force cascading Arabic into OTL.
      </p>
      <p class="t-body">
        John Hudson, TypeDrawers, July 31<br />
        typedrawers.com/discussion/comment/72005
      </p>
    </div>
    <Fragment slot="notes">
      <p>John Hudson, on talking with Eliyezer Kohen, one of the inventors of OpenType Layout.</p>
    </Fragment>
  </Slide>

  <!-- ── Why Nasta’liq ── -->

  <Slide section="Why Nasta’liq" label="Nasta’liq">
    <div class="c-1-4 stack">
      <h2 class="t-head">Why Nasta’liq</h2>
      <p class="t-small">
        A relatively nonlinear and freeform style of the Perso-Arabic script.
        A calligrapher can stack words, extend strokes, and move letters and
        dots to balance black and white space.
      </p>
      <p class="t-small">
        Of all the major styles it is the one worst served by OpenType.
      </p>
    </div>
    <Fig
      class="c-5-12 fig-right"
      src={mishkin09}
      alt="Nasta’liq calligraphy by Mishkín-Qalam."
      credit={`Calligraphy by Mishkín-Qalam (1826–1912). ${PD}`}
    />
  </Slide>

  <Slide section="Why Nasta’liq" label="Al-Fatiha: calligraphy vs Gulzar">
    <Fig
      class="c-1-12 fig-center"
      src={fatiha}
      alt="Al-Fatiha by Mir Emad Hassani on the left, the same lines set in Gulzar on the right."
      credit={`Left: Al-Fatiha by Mir Emad Hassani (1554–1615), National Museum of Iran. ${PD} Right: Gulzar.`}
    />
    <Fragment slot="notes">
      <p>You do not need to read Nasta’liq. Look for the differences between the two sides.</p>
    </Fragment>
  </Slide>

  <Slide section="Why Nasta’liq" label="Al-Fatiha: the first two lines">
    <div class="c-1-12 zoom-pair">
      <Fig
        class="fig-center"
        src={fatihaLinesGulzar}
        alt="The first two lines of Al-Fatiha set in Gulzar."
      />
      <Fig
        class="fig-center"
        src={fatihaLinesCalligraphy}
        alt="The same two lines in Mir Emad Hassani’s calligraphy."
      />
    </div>
    <Fragment slot="notes">
      <p>The same two lines, closer. Look at how the calligrapher stacks, stretches and spaces them.</p>
    </Fragment>
  </Slide>

  <Slide section="Why Nasta’liq" label="Gulzar in use">
    <img
      class="bleed"
      src={gulzarInUse.src}
      alt="A framed print of the bismillah set in Gulzar, hanging above a dresser."
    />
    <Fragment slot="notes">
      <p>This is what people buy and hang on the wall. The same line as the calligraphy, set as type.</p>
    </Fragment>
  </Slide>

  <Slide section="Demo" label="Live demo: the neural font">
    <div class="c-1-4 stack demo-side">
      <h2 class="t-head">This font is a neural network</h2>
      <p class="t-small">
        A small feed-forward network: Unicode and neighbors in, a signed
        distance field out.
      </p>
      <p class="t-small">
        1.36 million weights: 5.4 MB at 32 bits, 1.38 MB quantized to 8<br />
        No OpenType shaping at runtime<br />
        Rust, WASM, Linebender, img2bez
      </p>
      <table class="mono-table">
        <thead>
          <tr><th></th><th>OpenType</th><th>NeuralType</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Unit</td><td>Glyph in a box</td><td>Shape from context</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Behavior</td><td>GSUB, GPOS</td><td>Learned</td></tr>
          <tr><td>File</td><td>Outlines, tables</td><td>Weights</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Engine</td><td>HarfBuzz</td><td>NeuralType</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <div class="c-5-12 slide-demo ratio-3-2">
      <NeuralTypeDemo {...nastaliqDemo} />
    </div>
    <Fragment slot="notes">
      <ul>
        <li>Click in the demo. Type. Space separates joined letters.</li>
        <li>Try the sample list: نور على نور, درد عشق.</li>
        <li>Switch precision 32 / 16 / 8 bit.</li>
        <li>Esc to leave the demo, then → to continue.</li>
      </ul>
    </Fragment>
  </Slide>

  <!-- ── Measuring the teacher ── -->

  <!-- ── Dataset ── -->

  <!-- ── Signed-distance fields ── -->

  <!-- ── Model ── -->

  <Slide section="Training" label="Training on a local GPU">
    <div class="c-1-4 stack">
      <h2 class="t-head">Trained at home</h2>
      <p class="t-small">
        Distilled from Gulzar: 91,390 examples from 30,783 strings of one to
        three letters, 1,150 distinct shapes.
      </p>
      <p class="t-small">
        Rust and Candle on an NVIDIA RTX 2060 SUPER with 8 GB. About 100
        seconds per epoch.
      </p>
      <p class="t-small">
        Validation IoU 0.945 on held-out short strings.
      </p>
    </div>
    <Fig
      class="c-5-12 fig-right"
      src={train}
      alt="A terminal dashboard showing training progress, loss, validation IoU, and GPU use."
    />
    <Fragment slot="notes">
      <p>Training and inference both run locally. Independent designers can do this with full control of their data.</p>
      <p>IoU: overlap divided by union. 1.0 is identical. It measures shape, not position, and not new words.</p>
    </Fragment>
  </Slide>

  <Slide section="Rendering" label="Live demo: img2bez">
    <div class="c-1-4 stack">
      <h2 class="t-head">Field to outline</h2>
      <p class="t-small">
        A fast tracer draws while you type. When input settles, img2bez
        replaces it with a cleaner Bézier outline in a Web Worker.
      </p>
      <p class="t-small muted">
        Here img2bez traces a raster glyph. In the font it reads the field
        directly.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="c-5-12 slide-demo ratio-6-5" style="justify-self: end">
      <TraceDemo image="/demos/img2bez/a.png" glyph="a" unicode="0061" />
    </div>
    <Fragment slot="notes">
      <p>Hold space over the demo to flip structure and preview. Esc, then →.</p>
    </Fragment>
  </Slide>

  <!-- ── Results ── -->

  <!-- ── Beyond distillation (new for the talk) ── -->

  <Slide section="Beyond distillation" label="Possible, not the design">
    <div class="c-1-10 stack-lg">
      <p class="t-display">Distillation shows this is possible.</p>
      <p class="t-display muted">It is not the right design.</p>
    </div>
    <Fragment slot="notes">
      <p>The model copies a font that was built for movable-type thinking: one glyph in one box, with rules between boxes. Distilling it carries those limits into the network.</p>
    </Fragment>
  </Slide>

  <!-- ── A font from drawings (the editor and the first native model) ── -->

  <Slide section="Live" label="Runebender">
    <img
      class="bleed"
      src={endRunebender.src}
      alt="Runebender with a labeled bismillah and its preview."
    />
    <div class="c-1-9 over-bleed">
      <p class="t-display">Runebender</p>
    </div>
    <Fragment slot="notes">
      <p>Switch to Runebender now. About two minutes.</p>
    </Fragment>
  </Slide>

  <Slide section="A font from drawings" label="Live demo: the new font">
    <div class="c-1-4 stack">
      <p class="t-small">
        Trained from the labeled .nufo alone. No OpenType font, no starting
        weights.
      </p>
      <p class="t-small">
        Drag the end of <bdi>بب</bdi>. The font draws the <bdi>ب</bdi>
        between the short and long drawings.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="c-5-12 slide-demo ratio-3-2">
      <NeuralTypeDemo {...baBasicDemo} />
    </div>
    <Fragment slot="notes">
      <ul>
        <li>Drag the end node of بب to the left. The ب stretches.</li>
        <li>Esc to leave the demo, then → to continue.</li>
      </ul>
    </Fragment>
  </Slide>

  <Slide section="End" label="Links">
    <div class="c-1-10 stack-lg">
      <p class="t-display">Thank you</p>
      <p class="t-body">
        elih.net/blog/nastaliq-distilled<br />
        runebender.org<br />
        github.com/eliheuer/post-opentype<br />
        eli@elih.net
      </p>
    </div>
  </Slide>
</SlidesLayout>
